\details{
Given pings, each starts a map; given a map from either, it adds a layer drawn over the earlier ones, from the map's
pings and settings (\code{lon}, \code{lat}, \code{time}, \code{by}) unless \code{data} gives others. The result is an \code{\link[=rb_map]{rb_map()}}, shown when
printed: at the console in RStudio's Viewer (or a browser), a map printed on the same port replacing the last and
dropping its temporary tables. \code{\link[=rb_map_serve]{rb_map_serve()}} serves it on a port of one's choice.

Points: a numeric column is coloured on a continuous palette spread over its 1st to 99th percentile; any other
column, or with \code{discrete = TRUE}, takes a colour per value (the 12 most frequent; the rest grey). Several columns,
\code{c(speed, behaviour)}, are offered under "Colour by" in the page, the first shown. A point's radius is in metres,
so it grows as the map zooms in, from 1 pixel to \code{radius_max} pixels.

Track: each vessel's pings in time order, broken where they are more than \code{gap_h} hours apart. With a column, such
as \code{fishing_flow}, it breaks where the value changes too, each run coloured by its value (as the points are) and
ending at the next run's first fix, so the line stays joined. A second call adds a second track, by another column,
each one turned on and off in the page: \code{pings |> rb_map_track(fishing_flow) |> rb_map_track(provider)}.

Grouped pings, \code{group_by(trip)} or \code{group_by(trip, gear)}, give the page a control per group column above the
layers, each value with its number of pings: for a column of 12 values or fewer, chips that tick on and off, so
several show at once (setting and hauling); for one of more, a list to show one at a time; and buttons that step to
the previous and next. A choice shows those groups' pings and lines on every layer that has the column, fits the
view to them, and goes into the address. The groups cut the track too, so each group's line is its own.

The map opens on a dark base map. Each layer copies its pings into a temporary DuckDB table, as
\code{\link[=rb_map_tiles_table]{rb_map_tiles_table()}} does.
